6.8 Define your notation once
If you write \(\mathrm{d}N/\mathrm{d}t\) two hundred times, define it once. Put your macros in a file called _macros.tex at the top of the project:
\providecommand{\dd}{\mathrm{d}}
\newcommand{\dt}[1]{\frac{\mathrm{d}#1}{\mathrm{d}t}}
\providecommand{\ev}[1]{\mathbb{E}\!\left[#1\right]}
\providecommand{\var}[1]{\mathrm{Var}\!\left(#1\right)}
The [1] means “this macro takes one argument”, and #1 is where that argument lands. So \dt{N} expands to the whole derivative.
Then start every chapter that needs them with an include shortcode on the very first line:
{{&lt; include _macros.tex &gt;}}
Now $\dt{N}$ renders correctly in both outputs from a single definition: MathJax picks up the \newcommands in the browser, LaTeX picks them up in the PDF. This is exactly what the BMS book does.






TipWhy \providecommand rather than \newcommand



\providecommand only defines the macro if it does not already exist, so it cannot collide with something LaTeX already knows. Use it for anything with a plausibly common name; keep \newcommand for names you are sure are yours.


_macros.tex can also contain \usepackage{…} lines that only make sense for the PDF. MathJax simply ignores them, which is why one include is safe for both formats.






NoteA tidy pattern



Wrap the include in a hidden div so it never shows up as an empty line in the rendered page:
::: {.content-hidden}
$ {{&lt; include _macros.tex &gt;}} $
:::

7.2 Content that only exists in one format
An interactive simulation cannot exist on paper, and a full-page table of contents makes no sense on the web. Quarto’s native mechanism for this is conditional content:
::: {.content-visible when-format="html"}
&lt;the live simulation&gt;
:::

::: {.content-visible when-format="pdf"}
![Screenshot of the simulation](figures/sim-screenshot.png)

The interactive version is at &lt;https://…&gt;
:::
There is also .content-hidden when-format="…" if it reads better inverted.






WarningConditional content runs after the code does



The div hides output; it does not stop a chunk from executing. A chunk that emits HTML-only output should also be gated at the chunk level:
#| eval: !expr knitr::is_html_output()

7.3 Printing the web page
Students will hit Ctrl-P on a chapter. This is a different problem from the one above, and the distinction catches everybody out once.

7.3.1 The page furniture looks after itself
You do not have to hide the sidebar, the table of contents or the previous/next links. Quarto’s own CSS already does it:
/* from Quarto's bundled bootstrap css */
@media print {
  :root { font-size: 11pt }
  #quarto-sidebar, #TOC, .nav-page { display: none }
  .fixed-top { position: relative }
}
This applies to any Quarto HTML output, book or website. If you have ever seen a Quarto page print with its menu attached, it was almost certainly not Quarto output at all.


7.3.2 The pattern the BMS book uses
This is the one worth copying. Every interactive embed in that book is a pair: a screenshot with a short caption for paper, and the live thing for screen.
:::{.print-only}
&lt;center&gt;*Interactieve content beschikbaar op de website:*
![](figuren/interactive_plife.png){fig-align=center}
&lt;/center&gt;
:::

:::{.screen-only}
```{=html}
&lt;iframe width="100%" height="550"
  src="https://jsfiddle.net/…/embedded/result" title="Simulation"&gt;&lt;/iframe&gt;
```
:::
Two things make it work. The printed version is not an apology for a missing figure — it is a real screenshot plus a line telling the reader where the live version is. And because it is CSS rather than when-format, it applies to the web page itself: a student who prints chapter 4 gets the screenshots, and the same file on screen keeps the simulations.
The cost is that you have to take the screenshots. Thirty-odd of them, in the BMS book’s case. There is no way around that — a browser cannot photograph an iframe for you.


7.3.3 What you do have to handle
The content. Two helper classes, in an @media print block in your SCSS:
.screen-only { display: block; }
.print-only  { display: none; }

@media print {
  .screen-only { display: none !important; }
  .print-only  { display: block !important; border: 1px dashed #999; padding: 1em; }

  iframe { display: none !important; }      // prints as an empty rectangle

  h1, h2, h3 { break-after: avoid; }        // no heading orphaned at a page foot
  figure, table, pre, .callout { break-inside: avoid; }

  a[href^="http"]::after {                  // paper cannot be clicked
    content: " (" attr(href) ")";
    font-size: 0.75em;
  }
}
All of that is already in this book’s uu.scss, so ::: {.screen-only} and ::: {.print-only} work straight away — see the two embeds in Chapter 8, which use exactly this pattern.






TipGoing further: a two-column print layout





The BMS book squeezes a lot onto paper with column-count: 2 inside its @media print block, plus smaller type and forced full-width figures. Worth copying if students print whole chapters — but check your equations and wide code blocks, which do not enjoy narrow columns.









CautionHistorical note: print_friendly.html





Earlier versions of the BMS book used a hand-written &lt;script&gt; that swapped every &lt;iframe id="iframeToReplace"&gt; for a link on window.onbeforeprint and reloaded the page afterwards. It was clever, and it predates Quarto having good native tools.
It is retired: the file survives only as an unused copy in Boek/scripts/, and no live chapter includes it. Print handling now rests entirely on the CSS above plus content-visible.
Don’t teach it as a pattern. It relies on a magic element id, it reloads the page after printing, and content-visible when-format does the same job properly.

```{=html}
&lt;iframe src="…" width="100%" height="600"
        style="border:none; border-radius:8px;"&gt;
&lt;/iframe&gt;
```







ImportantIf your book also makes a PDF



An iframe cannot exist on paper. Wrap every one of them in ::: {.content-visible when-format="html"} and give the PDF a screenshot and a link instead — see Chapter 7. Otherwise the PDF gets a mysterious blank gap.

8.4 External: a video
Do not hand-write an iframe for YouTube. Quarto has a shortcode that does it properly, including on phones:
{{&lt; video https://www.youtube.com/embed/wo9vZccmqwc &gt;}}
